#chapter[Integral theta lifts and a common period]

== Automorphic forms on a finite genus <c12s01>

Let $E slash F$ be the CM extension with its maximal real subfield, and let $X$ be a Hermitian space over $E$. Its unitary group $U(X)$ consists of $E$-linear maps preserving the Hermitian form. Suppose $X$ is definite at every real place. With an integral lattice and an open compact stabilizer $K$ at the finite adeles, the double quotient
$ U(X)(F) backslash U(X)(𝔸_(F,f)) slash K $
is finite. In a lattice model the points are isometry classes in a genus, with the chosen level structure. A scalar-valued automorphic form is a function on this finite set. A form of algebraic weight takes values in a representation of the compact archimedean groups, subject to the stabilizer condition. A corresponding space of integral forms is obtained by specifying stable lattices in the coefficient representations.

A class represented by a lattice $P$ carries mass proportional to $frac(1,|op("Aut")(P)|)$. These masses weight the scalar inner product. They enter both the adjoint and the determinant when an adelic integral is written as a matrix between integral lattices.

== A finite quotient with stabilizers <c12s02>

The spaces in the period identity are easier to recognize after doing a computation on a finite quotient. Let a finite group $G$ act on a finite set $X$. Choose representatives $x_i$ of its orbits, and write $H_i$ for their stabilizers. A $G$-invariant scalar function is a vector indexed by $i$. Integration with respect to counting measure on $X$ divided by $|G|$ gives
$ frac(1,|G|) sum_(x in X) f(x) overline(g(x))
      = sum_i frac(1,|H_i|) f(x_i) overline(g(x_i)). $
Indeed the orbit of $x_i$ has $frac(|G|,|H_i|)$ points. This formula even makes sense for a disjoint union of group actions in which each stabilizer is finite. It is the elementary model for mass on the definite genus. At adelic level there is a Haar-volume factor set by the normalization of the compact subgroup. After it has been fixed, its contribution is shared by every term.

For a coefficient representation $V$, use the convention $f(g x)=g f(x)$. The value $f(x_i)$ then lies in the fixed space $V^(H_i)$. Conversely a choice of a fixed vector in every such space extends to an equivariant function, because two ways of writing $g x_i$ differ by an element of $H_i$. If $V$ carries a unitary inner product the same mass formula holds for the vector inner product. A lattice $V_0$ preserved by $G$ yields the integral lattice of possible values $V_0^(H_i)$. At primes dividing the stabilizer order the orthogonal projector $|H_i|^(-1)sum_(h in H_i) h$ can have denominators. Existence of an invariant rational vector and integrality of an invariant projector are therefore separate questions.

#example[An invariant line of index two][Let an involution exchange the two coordinates of $ℤ^2$. The fixed lattice is $ℤ(1,1)$ and its orthogonal complement is $ℤ(1,-1)$. Their sum has index two. Projection onto the fixed line takes $(a,b)$ to $(frac(a+b,2),frac(a+b,2))$. At odd primes it preserves the local integer lattice. At two, evaluating on $(1,0)$ shows a denominator. The fixed line itself is saturated, since the quotient by it is torsion free. Thus saturation of a submodule does not alone control the denominator of the *orthogonal* projection onto it.]

A second calculation exhibits the measure in an adjoint. Let the two source masses be $1$ and $1/2$ and let the single target mass be $1$. Consider the kernel with values $K(y,1)=K(y,2)=1$. Integrating the kernel against a vector gives the row $T(f_1,f_2)=f_1+f_2/2$. Its adjoint for the weighted inner products is $T^(*)(g)=(g,g)$, since the source inner product already assigns the second coordinate weight $1/2$. The composite on the one-dimensional range is multiplication by $3/2$. Giving the source two equal masses would instead produce two. Thus a mass can reappear in a norm identity despite having disappeared from the coordinate vector of the adjoint. For longer chains this is one reason to choose the Haar measures before identifying the two descriptions of a period.

In the arithmetic setting the sets indexing double cosets are of the form $U(W)(F) backslash U(W)(𝔸_(F,f)) slash K$. Their points can be described using lattices and their local identifications with a chosen reference lattice. At the real places the weights specify a representation, so the values of a definite automorphic form at a point obey exactly a stabilizer condition. The full construction also uses indefinite spaces. There one works with actual adelic integrals, their convergence, and their comparison with holomorphic or algebraic models. Finiteness of a definite class set cannot justify interchanging integrations on those larger spaces. The conditions on convergence and matching below supply that additional step.

== A kernel, an adjoint, and an iteration <c12s03>

For a unitary dual pair, a Schwartz function $varphi$ in a Weil representation produces a theta kernel by summing its transforms over rational vectors. In schematic notation
$ Theta_(varphi)(g,h)=sum_v (omega(g,h)varphi)(v). $
Pairing this kernel in $h$ with an automorphic form produces a form in $g$. The choice of splitting characters and local measures is part of the definition. At infinity polynomials in the Fock model select specified algebraic weights. At finite places integral test functions describe lattice and level conditions. Matching these descriptions gives actual linear maps in the chosen spaces of forms.

The chain has even length $m$. On one side its ranks are
$ N_j=k+j(t+1), quad 0<=j<=m. $
The companion side uses $r=k+m-1$ and ranks $r+j t$. Here $k$ is even, $t$ is odd, $m+3<=k$, and $3m<t$. At the last step the two ranks differ by one. The arrays of dominant weights interlace. Their strict gaps allow selected characters to be varied while retaining the branching and local types used in the construction. In writing $T_m dots T_1$ we suppress auxiliary pairings and level-changing maps, all of which carry fixed normalization factors.

An identity for two pairings of the same theta kernel is known as a seesaw identity. On a finite quotient one already sees the algebra. If $T f(y)=sum_x K(y,x)f(x)mu_x$, pairing against $g(y)$ gives a double sum. Summing in the other order gives the adjoint pairing of $f$ against $T^*g$. In the adelic identity a common Weil representation supplies the two restrictions of the kernel. The convergence and local matching theorems justify the interchange and the choice of test functions. The Rallis inner product formula and the local computations determine the associated norm data.

== A numerical two-step chain and its strict gaps <c12s04>

The ranks permit a calculation in which every integer can be checked. In the notation of the source put $m=2$, $k=8$, $t=15$ and $n=4$, so $f=2n=8$ and $r=k+m-1=9$. The choice $n=4$ also meets the minimum $2^e<=n$ at two-power depth $e=2$, the shallowest depth permitted by the host. #src("Formalism.SharpRestriction.RamificationFrobenius.TotientGaloisClasses.FrameFacts")[The host order at a given depth.] These numbers satisfy the parity and elementary rank hypotheses. In detail $m+3<=k$, $r+5<=t$, $3m<t$, $m<=f<=k$, and $m^2<=k+m(t+1)$. The successive pairs of ranks are

#align(center)[#table(columns: (auto,auto,auto,auto), align: center, inset: (x: 6pt,y: 3pt), stroke: none,
  table.hline(), [Step $j$], [$N_j$], [$r+j t$], [Difference],
  table.hline(stroke: 0.4pt), [0], [8], [9], [$-1$], [1], [24], [24], [0], [2], [40], [39], [1], table.hline())]

The two sides gain sixteen and fifteen dimensions per step, respectively. In general $N_j-(r+j t)=j-m+1$. Thus the ranks tie one step before the end and differ by one at the endpoint. The intermediate signature on the fixed rank-$r$ side at step $j$ is $(k+j-1,m-j)$. For the example these pairs for $j=1,2$ are $(8,1)$ and $(9,0)$. The endpoint is definite. More generally the middle step $j=m/2$ has the prescribed initial signature $(k+(m/2-1),m/2)$. This convention explains why $m$ must be even and why the expression $r=k+m-1$ contains a subtraction. These are legal *rank data*. They do not construct the family of fields, large weights and prime pairs for an arbitrary analytic window.

The weight inequalities can also be met explicitly at the level of the parameter record. At a single embedding take
$ u=(15/2,13/2,11/2,9/2,7/2,5/2,3/2,1/2), quad v=(7,6,5,4,3,2,1). $
They strictly interlace. For the two steps take $b_1=1$, $e_1=10$, $b_2=19$, $e_2=28$. The smallest $u$ equals $1/2>-b_1$. Both differences $e_j-b_j$ and the intermediate difference $b_2-e_1$ are nine, which equals $frac(t+3,2)$. The half-integrality of the $u$’s and integrality of the other entries give the correct parity for the normalized parameters. These sample numbers illustrate the inequalities in the abstract rank-and-weight data. The actual top weights, which must also meet the exponentially large windows, are chosen later in the arithmetic instance.

The same parameters occur with three explicit rank shifts. For a step $j>=1$ write
$ s_j=e_j-frac(N_(j-1),2), quad s'_j=e_j-frac(N_j,2), $
$ w_j^G=e_j-frac(k,2)-frac((2j-1)(t+1),2). $
The first two differ by $frac(t+1,2)$. Substituting $N_(j-1)=k+(j-1)(t+1)$ shows that $w_j^G=s_j-frac(j(t+1),2)$. For the numerical chain the pairs $(s_j,s'_j)$ are $(6,-2)$ and $(16,8)$, and $w_1^G=-2$, $w_2^G=0$. The sign of a shifted parameter need not agree with that of the input $e_j$. Later the positive function $w_(j)(tau)$ entering the arithmetic prime sum is the chosen $e_j$ at the embedding, without this representation-theoretic rank shift. Confusing the two would change the arithmetic argument $frac(a_t,w_(j)(tau))$. #src("Formalism.LocalRingCardinality.Params")[Ranks, interlacing, and the three shifts.] #src("Formalism.LocalRingCardinality.DataParams₂")[The numerical weight at an embedding.]

Why should interlacing enter the choice of such a map? A low-rank branching computation gives an answer. The irreducible polynomial representation $op("Sym")^(d)(ℂ^2)$ has the monomial basis $X^(d-i)Y^i$, $0<=i<=d$. Restrict to the subgroup acting on $X$ by $z$ and fixing $Y$. The possible characters are $z^d,z^(d-1),dots,1$, each once. After multiplying the original representation by a determinant power $b$, an irreducible highest weight $(a,b)$ with $a-b=d$ restricts to characters of weights $a,a-1,dots,b$. This is the first branching inequality. In higher rank, the adjacent entries of one dominant weight place upper and lower bounds on each entry of the restricting weight. In the normalized infinitesimal-character coordinates those weak inequalities can appear as strict interlacing with a half-unit shift. The strict gaps in the chain leave room for perturbations of character data while preserving the selected constituents. The sample $u_i,v_i$ show explicitly how one integer can fit between adjacent half-integers.

== The oscillator kernel in two realizations <c12s05>

At a finite place one starts with a Schwartz function, meaning a locally constant compactly supported function on the local vector space. A typical unramified vector is the indicator of an integral lattice. With Haar measure giving a lattice $P$ volume one and an additive character with respect to which $P$ is self-dual, the Fourier transform of $1_P$ is again $1_P$. In fact the integral at a dual vector $y$ is one when the character $x mapsto psi(chevron.l x,y chevron.r)$ is trivial on $P$. Otherwise translating $x$ by a single element on which that character is nontrivial forces the integral to vanish. In the general case the formula is $hat(1_P)=vol(P)1_(P^sharp)$, where $P^sharp$ is the dual lattice for the pairing and character. Local matching has to track this dependence on all three choices.

At an archimedean place the corresponding vectors include a Gaussian multiplied by a polynomial. For the one-dimensional model use $g(x)=exp(-pi x^2)$. Then $g'(x)=-2pi x g(x)$ and $g''(x)=(4pi^2 x^2-2pi) g(x)$. Polynomial times Gaussian vectors are stable under differentiation and multiplication by $x$. In many variables the monomials, differentiated and projected onto a constituent of a chosen weight, select the polynomial degree required by a representation. This calculation explains the roles of the polynomial model and the derivative orders. It does not supply the large-rank local matching theorem. The latter identifies quotient representations and relates the finite Schwartz data on the two sides while the polynomial data vary.

Here is the relevant content of that theorem in the release. Fix the global data, a standard additive character, the infinite-place data, three Hermitian spaces $W,W_1,X$ with their frames, and exponent functions on the infinite places. There is a complex vector space $P_r$ and two *surjective* linear maps onto it, one from the polynomial representation for $(W,X)$ and one from that for $(W_1,X)$. For compatible splitting characters with the prescribed infinite exponents the theorem supplies matched Weil representations. For each such matched datum there is a finite-data quotient $P_f$ and an identification
$ P_r ⊗ P_f ≃ Pi $
with the common representation used to compare test vectors. Every element of $P_f$ can be represented by admissible finite data on either side. The image of the test vector with polynomial $P$ and finite part $f$ on the first side is the tensor of the images $q(P)$ and $r(f)$ under the identification. The analogous rule holds on the second side using $q'$ and $r'$. Both surjectivities and both tensor formulas are part of the input. If two polynomial models had the same dimension but mapped into different quotients, there would be no such comparison. #src("Development.AbelianVarietyTheory.Statements.HeckeThetaIntegrals.ThetaIntegralsAgree")[LocalMatching.]

One elementary consequence of this formulation is worth spelling out. For an allowed $P,f$ in the first model, find $P'$ in the second with $q'(P')=q(P)$ by surjectivity. Find an admissible $f'$ representing $r(f)$ in $P_f$ by the finite assertion. The images of the corresponding *simple tensors* now agree in $Pi$. A general algebraic tensor is a finite sum of simple tensors, so linearity extends equality of images to such sums. Equality of quotient images does not immediately give equality of the adelic integrals. One still must know that the integral factors through the relevant relations and that the representative being integrated is admissible.

To see the role of these relations, let a group $H$ act on a vector space $A$. A linear functional $I$ invariant under $H$ vanishes on expressions $h a-a$ and therefore descends to the quotient by their span. If a different integral is initially defined on a smaller subspace, a coset representative may have to be replaced by an integrable one before applying it. In the release the equality of theta integrals has exactly that order. For fixed Haar measures and fundamental domains for $W$ and $W_1$, and matched data, there is a nonzero scalar $c$. For every algebraic vector $Phi'$ on the second side there is an algebraic vector $Phi_0$ differing from it by a finite relation, with the required integrability. For every algebraic vector $Phi$ on the first side having the same quotient image as $Phi_0$, the two theta integrals agree up to $c$. The scalar is chosen before the test vectors. Replacing it by a scalar allowed to depend on $Phi$ would eliminate the linear information needed for a period map. #src("Development.AbelianVarietyTheory.Statements.HeckeThetaIntegrals.ThetaIntegralsAgree")[ThetaIntegralsAgree.]

There is also a convergence hypothesis with a concrete rank test. In the appropriate standard Weil model, if $M$ has a real signature $(op("rank")(M)-p,p)$ at one of its real places and $op("rank")(X)<2(op("rank")(M)-p)$, the stated convergence theorem gives integrability for the admissible theta vectors with the chosen Haar fundamental domain. At a positive-definite place in the displayed ordering, $p=0$ and the right side is twice the rank. At a negative-definite place $p=op("rank")(M)$ and this sufficient test gives no positive range. One must exhibit a place with the indicated signature and a sufficiently large positive index. This illustrates why a proof passing through indefinite spaces must specify which convergent pairing is used. The actual equality theorem is formulated for matched models and their allowable representatives. #src("Development.AbelianVarietyTheory.Statements.HeckeThetaIntegrals.ThetaIntegralsAgree")[WeilConvergence.]

== Why a Bessel period is needed <c12s06>

The construction varies a word $u$ of auxiliary choices along the lifts. If the endpoint period lived in a different unrelated target for each word, divisibility for several words could not constrain one lattice. The seesaw gives a Bessel period, a linear functional obtained by integrating along a subgroup with its prescribed character, in a common target. More explicitly, the rational period maps $B_u$ and their Bessel descriptions satisfy
$ B_(u)(v,F)=c_u P_(u)(U_u v,V F), quad c_u!=0, $
with $U_u$ unitary on the source and $V$ unitary on the common factor and independent of $u$. The rational comparison identifies the complexified bilinear form with the chosen period. Integrality enters through a separate image inclusion into the dual lattice. The single target isometry preserves the mixed inner products between different words, and the scalar and the map norm enter their height estimates.

#example[A two-column calculation][Let an integral linear map send a basis to $(2,0)$ and $(0,3)$ in $ℤ^2$. Its image has index six, and the square root of its real Gram determinant is also six. If the columns are changed to $(2,0)$ and $(3,0)$, their individual norms provide no positive area at all. In the theta construction control of mutual intersections and total rank serves the purpose illustrated by this loss of area.]

At the two auxiliary Hecke places, the local types fix factors that recur at every stage. Elsewhere a loss per lift can grow with $m$. Comparing many lifts and extracting their common integral divisors allows the next chapter to estimate the gain.

== Computing a definite theta coefficient <c12s07>

There is a small definite theta series whose coefficients can be computed in two quite different ways. Consider the lattice $Λ=ℤ[i]$ with quadratic norm $N(a+i b)=a^2+b^2$. For a homogeneous polynomial $P$ on this lattice and a formal variable $Q$, put
$ Θ_(P)(Q)=sum_(z in Λ) P(z) Q^(N(z)). $
Every coefficient is a finite sum because $N$ is positive definite. Nothing about convergence of an indefinite theta integral is needed here. For $P=1$ the coefficient $r_(2)(n)$ counts representations of $n$ by two squares with both order and signs. For nonzero $n$ the unit group acts freely by multiplication on the representing lattice points. Thus $frac(r_(2)(n),4)$ counts the orbits of these points. The constant coefficient, by contrast, comes from zero, whose stabilizer consists of all four units. This is a coefficient-level example of the different weights in integration over a genus.

Factorization in the Euclidean domain $ℤ[i]$ computes $r_(2)(n)$. Write the prime factorization of a positive integer $n$ in the form
$ n=2^a product_(p equiv 1 space "mod" space 4) p^(b_p)
       product_(q equiv 3 space "mod" space 4) q^(c_q). $
The rational prime two is the norm of $1+i$ and has only one prime above it up to associates. An odd prime congruent to three modulo four stays prime in the Gaussian integers. Indeed a square root of $-1$ modulo such a prime would have order four in its multiplicative group, impossible since four does not divide $q-1$. An odd prime congruent to one modulo four splits as $pi_p overline(pi_p)$. For the latter assertion take an element of order four in the cyclic group of nonzero residues. The quotient $ℤ[i] slash p ℤ[i]$ then has zero divisors, as $X^2+1$ has two distinct roots modulo $p$. Thus $p$ is reducible in the Gaussian integers. Taking norms shows its factors can be chosen with norm $p$, and conjugation interchanges them.

If $N(z)=n$, an inert prime $q$ in $z$ contributes twice its exponent to $c_q$. Any odd $c_q$ eliminates all representations. At two there is only one choice of exponent for $1+i$. At a split prime there are $b_p+1$ choices, namely the possible exponents of $pi_p$ in $z$ between zero and $b_p$, with the complementary exponent assigned to $overline(pi_p)$. These choices are independent by unique factorization. Finally the four units yield distinct nonzero elements. Consequently
$ r_(2)(n)=4 product_(p equiv 1 space "mod" space 4)(b_p+1) $
if every $c_q$ is even, and it is zero otherwise. For $n=25$ there are twelve points. Four are the unit multiples of $5$ and the other eight come from the two conjugate squares of primes above five. For $n=65$ there are sixteen points because both five and thirteen split. At $n=45$, the square of the inert prime three forces divisibility of $z$ by three but leaves the eight possibilities above five. Replacing the exponent two of three by one, as in $n=15$, kills the coefficient.

The calculation works with a weight as well. Take $P(z)=z^h$ under the chosen complex embedding. Rotating by $i$ multiplies the weight by $i^h$ while permuting every shell of fixed norm. A positive-index coefficient therefore vanishes unless four divides $h$. For $h=4$ and $n=5$ there are the orbits of $2+i$ and $2-i$. Since $(2+i)^2=3+4i$, its fourth power is $-7+24i$. Each of its four associates gives the same fourth power. Combining the two orbits gives
$ [Q^5]Θ_(z^4)=4(-7+24i)+4(-7-24i)=-56. $
The sign is meaningful even though the unweighted shell has eight points. A positive counting formula alone loses the phases contributed by the weight. In the unitary period, the coefficient similarly starts with lattice vectors satisfying a Gram-matrix condition and a specified polynomial test vector. Local matching controls that polynomial as well as the support of the vectors. Higher-rank orbital integrals and the indefinite comparison supply further information absent from this definite binary example.

== Projecting a character component at an auxiliary level <c12s08>

The word data also select local characters. A finite-group calculation describes the integral issue in making such a selection. Let $C$ be a finite abelian group of order $d$ acting on a vector space over a characteristic-zero field containing all the character values. For a character $chi$ define
$ e_(chi)=frac(1,d) sum_(a in C) chi(a)^(-1) a. $
The formula belongs to the group algebra. Multiplication on the left by $b in C$ sends it to $chi(b)e_(chi)$ after a change of variable. Every vector in its image consequently has character $chi$. Conversely it fixes every vector already transforming by that character. It follows that it is a projector onto the exact character subspace. For two distinct characters $chi$ and $psi$, apply it to a $psi$-eigenvector. The result is the mean of the nontrivial character $psi chi^(-1)$ times that vector, which vanishes. To see the vanishing, choose $b$ where the character differs from one and reindex the sum by multiplication by $b$. This proves orthogonality over the coefficient field.

The integral denominator can survive even when all character values are units. Take $C$ cyclic of order four with generator $g$, the coefficient ring $ℤ[i]$, and $chi(g)=i$. Then
$ 4 e_(chi)=1-i g-g^2+i g^3. $
On the regular lattice with basis $1,g,g^2,g^3$, the image of the basis vector $1$ has four coordinates with denominator four. The $chi$-eigenline itself has an integral generator $v=1-i g-g^2+i g^3$, and multiplication by $g$ sends $v$ to $i v$. The projector is the identity on this integral generator. Thus knowing that the eigenspace has an integral generator does not settle which multiples of that generator lie in the *projection* of a larger lattice. Over the local ring at an odd rational prime four is invertible. At the prime $1+i$ it has valuation four when the valuation of $1+i$ is normalized to one. This simple change of level can therefore create a cost concentrated at a single prime.

For a unitary action the same operator is orthogonal for an invariant Hermitian metric. Indeed adjunction takes $a$ to $a^(-1)$ and conjugates the character coefficient. Reindexing by inversion recovers $e_(chi)$. Passing to the dual space, however, replaces an eigencharacter by its inverse. If $a v=chi(a)v$ and the functional $lambda$ is transported by $(a lambda)(v)=lambda(a^(-1)v)$, the induced value on that line transforms by $chi(a)^(-1)$. A bilinear pairing of a $chi$ line with a $psi$ line can therefore be invariant with nonzero value only when $chi psi=1$. A Hermitian pairing produces a conjugate on one side instead. These conventions have concrete implications when the archimedean isometry and the rational period are identified. One must know which side is dualized and which local character a word specifies. The prime-by-prime divisibility established later concerns the actual selected integral operator. The projector calculation exhibits a mechanism that such divisibility estimates must allow for, without identifying the particular word maps with the simple regular-representation projector.

== The common period as a map into a dual lattice <c12s09>

We give names to the spaces because a sign or an adjoint becomes difficult to check in a statement about scalar periods alone. Let $K$ be the coefficient number field, $R$ the fixed rational space with lattice $R_0$, and $N_u$ the rational space belonging to the word $u$, with lattice $N_(u,0)$. The rational operator is
$ B_(K,u):N_u arrow.r R^∨, quad z mapsto (y mapsto B_(K,u)(z,y)). $
Thus the common target is the dual of the same $R$ for every word. Its integral lattice is $R_0^∨={lambda in R^∨:lambda(R_0) subset.eq cal("O")_K}$. The separate integral condition on this rational map, called #raw("OneMapIntoCC"), is $B_(K,u)(N_(u,0)) subset.eq R_0^∨$. Equivalently, it defines an integral bilinear pairing on $N_(u,0) times R_0$. To prove the equivalence, restrict each functional to $R_0$ in one direction and extend scalars to $K$ in the other. Fullness of the lattices ensures that a rational linear functional is determined by its values on the lattice. #src("Formalism.LocalRingCardinality.DataArith")[The common target and integral restriction.]

After applying the coefficient embedding, there are spaces $M_u$ and $cal("E")$ over the complex numbers. The rational comparison explicitly says that evaluating the complex period on the images of $z in N_u$ and $y in R$ gives the coefficient embedding of $B_(K,u)(z,y)$. There is then a *single* unitary isometry $V$ of $cal("E")$ such that for each word one can find a nonzero scalar $a_u$ and a unitary isometry $U_u$ of $M_u$ with
$ B_(u)(phi,F)=a_u P_(u)(U_u phi,V F) $
for all pairs of vectors. The Bessel expression is made from an inner product between the image of $F$ in a common Hilbert space and the image of $phi$ under the word's period operator. The maps used to embed the two inputs include the conjugation conventions needed to realize the bilinear rational pairing. The scalar and the source isometry can change with $u$. The target isometry cannot. The Bessel identity records the rational comparison and these quantifiers. Its statement contains no assertion that the integral image lies in the dual lattice. That assertion is provided by the separate division input. A scalar can alter the finite-place content without changing proportionality at the complex embedding. #src("Formalism.LocalRingCardinality.RhoDetPrimeEqMuTildeMulChiPow")[The rational and Bessel evaluations.] #src("Formalism.SharpRestriction.GlobalTheoremInputs")[BesselId.]

Why is a common target isometry stronger than a collection of per-word isometries? Consider the two maps from $ℂ$ to $ℂ^2$ taking $1$ to $e_1$ and $e_2$. Their image lines are orthogonal. A single unitary of $ℂ^2$ sends them to a new orthogonal pair. Two separately chosen target unitaries can send both vectors to $e_1$. Each map retains norm one, whereas the mixed inner product changes from zero to one. Bounds for *individual* norms therefore transfer under separate changes of coordinates. A mixed trace bound transfers only when the target identifications are tied together. This explains the outer existential quantifier for $V$.

The behavior of the scalar is equally concrete. For $B=a P$ the positive operator $B B^*$ equals $|a|^2 P P^*$. Assume $a$ and $P$ are nonzero. The squared Hilbert–Schmidt norm of $B$ is $tr(B B^*)=|a|^2 tr(P P^*)$. Dividing the map $B$ by its Hilbert–Schmidt norm before composing it with its adjoint gives the normalized positive operator $frac(P P^*,tr(P P^*))$. Its trace is one, irrespective of the scalar. The corresponding unnormalized integral height still records the scalar. The proof uses relative traces to control intersections of complex images, while integrality and the norm comparison keep track of scalar factors for the rational maps. The comparison between the two uses the same period at both stages.

There is a useful way to check the scalar ambiguity in an equality of period maps. Suppose $B_1,B_2:N arrow.r R^∨$ are defined over the same field $K$, with $B_2=c B_1$. If $B_1$ is nonzero, choose a lattice vector $x$ and a lattice vector $y$ on which its pairing is nonzero. Such vectors exist since the lattices span their rational spaces. Then $c=frac(B_(2)(x,y),B_(1)(x,y))$. Every other pair for which the denominator is nonzero must give the same ratio. Equivalently the cross product of the two evaluations vanishes for every two pairs. This observation can detect a mismatch of the maps, but verifying one nonzero value alone cannot prove proportionality on a higher-dimensional space. The matching theorem is what supplies the uniform scalar.

For example, on $ℤ^2 times ℤ$ take pairings represented by the rows $(5,10)$ and $(6,12)$. Both pair integrally on the indicated lattices, and over the rationals their ratio is $6/5$. At the prime five the first row has a common factor of five and the second has none. At two and three the direction of the difference reverses. Multiplication by the scalar has shifted the local content of the period exactly as the principal fractional ideal $(6/5)$ prescribes. At the real place its norm is also multiplied by $6/5$. Over a number field, each complex embedding uses the corresponding absolute value of the scalar. Prescribing an archimedean isometry and an integral normalization constrains these changes. Even when a global height comparison makes the total change from a rational rescaling cancel by the product formula, the assertion of divisibility at an individual place still uses the chosen normalization.

For an exterior power of order $s$, multiplying an operator by $c$ multiplies the induced operator by $c^s$. At a finite place this adds $s$ times the valuation of $c$ to each corresponding nonzero minor. A scalar common to every entry can therefore produce a large apparent change of determinant as the tested rank grows.

== Counting weights while the rank changes <c12s10>

For a strictly decreasing tuple $u=(u_1,dots,u_k)$ define
$ Delta(u)=product_(i<j) (u_i-u_j), quad D(u)=frac(Delta(u), product_(i=0)^(k-1)(i!)). $
When $u$ is the appropriately shifted highest weight, $D(u)$ is the Weyl dimension. The denominator can be recognized without representation theory. For the decreasing tuple $(k-1,k-2,dots,0)$ the product of the differences equals $product_(j=1)^(k-1) j!$ because the differences ending in a given position run from one through the index of that position minus one. Adding a common constant to every $u_i$ leaves $D(u)$ unchanged. In the representation the corresponding operation is a determinant twist; it changes the central character without changing the dimension.

For rank two, $D(u)=u_1-u_2$. A highest weight $(a,b)$ acquires the strict shifted entries $(a+1,b)$ and gives $a-b+1$, as the monomial calculation above predicted. For rank three, the highest weight $(2,1,0)$ has shifted entries $(4,2,0)$ and dimension $frac(2 dot 4 dot 2,2)=8$. Consider now two different rank-three shifted tuples, $(4,2,0)$ and $(5,2,0)$. Increasing just one endpoint by one multiplies the dimension by $(3/2)(5/4)=15/8$. In the actual highest-weight arrays, varying just a few entries produces a product of such ratios, one per pair involving a moved coordinate. Taking logs turns these into precisely the differences of log Vandermonde determinants used in counting the words.

The mass of a genus changes with the rank as well. The following algebraic form of the count shows where the period and mass normalizations enter. Suppose the model at rank $r$ has positive mass $M_r$, normalized archimedean scale $v>0$, and local positive correction $kappa_r$. For $k<r<=k+m-1$, let its mass recursion be
$ frac(M_r,M_(r-1)) =
    ((r-1)! (v/pi)^r)^f (2v)^(-f/2) kappa_r. $
Use one strictly decreasing tuple per embedding, with $f$ embeddings. Multiplying the mass by the $f$ Weyl dimensions introduces a factorial denominator for each tuple. In taking the quotient between two successive ranks the factor $((r-1)!)^f$ from the mass cancels the new factorials in these denominators. Telescoping and taking logs between ranks $k$ and $r_*=k+m-1$ now gives the change of log mass times dimension as the sum of four terms,

+ The sum over the embeddings of the changes in $log Delta$ between the final and initial tuples.
+ The period term $f[(m-1)k+frac(m(m-1),2)] log(v/pi)$.
+ The normalization term $-(m-1)(f/2)log(2v)$.
+ The sum of $log kappa_r$ for $k<r<=k+m-1$.

To check the period coefficient, sum the integers $k+1,dots,k+m-1$. Their sum is $(m-1)k+frac(m(m-1),2)$. The normalization term counts one contribution from each of the $m-1$ rank increments. When $m=2$ the recursion contains just rank $k+1$, whose factorial is $k!$. When $m=1$ all increments disappear and the Vandermonde difference remains between two tuples of equal length. The application uses even $m>=2$. Keeping the formal endpoint also verifies the telescoping identity. #src("Formalism.BehrendSets.StatementE")[The precise mass-and-dimension identity.]

In particular, a constant factor per embedding cannot always be swept into a bounded error. Its logarithm is multiplied by the number of embeddings and by the number of steps; it can also be multiplied by a large weight when the log count is inserted into a degree. Thus the occurrences of $v/pi$ in the count and of $pi/v$ in the period normalization must be compared exactly. A scalar normalization on a single fixed finite-dimensional vector space is harmless for deciding whether a map vanishes. A varying family of determinant measurements is sensitive to it. Chapter 14 follows these constants through the logarithmic inequality until they cancel.

== Factoring local divisors through a chain <c12s11>

Let $D$ be a discrete valuation ring with uniformizer $ϖ$. Suppose $L_0,dots,L_m$ are lattices over $D$ and each linear map $T_j$ satisfies $T_(j)(L_(j-1)) subset.eq ϖ^(g_j)L_j$, with integer $g_j>=0$. Then
$ T_m dots T_(1)(L_0) subset.eq ϖ^(g_1+ dots +g_m)L_m. $
For the induction, if an input to $T_j$ lies in $ϖ^a L_(j-1)$, linearity puts its image in $ϖ^a T_(j)(L_(j-1))$. This proof works also for rectangular matrices. One can retain a common factor in the full map without taking a determinant of the individual steps. Indeed a rectangular matrix has no determinant, and its maximal minors can transform more subtly under composition.

When a new integral model $L'_m$ satisfies $ϖ^h L_m subset.eq L'_m$, the same statement in $L'_m$ may lose $h$ units of exponent: if $a>=h$, then $ϖ^a L_m subset.eq ϖ^(a-h) L'_m$. If a different lattice is used at every step, losses can accumulate. This elementary inclusion gives the sign in the expression “sum of step gains less a replacement cost.” The true local statement keeps track of partial rank losses as well as common divisibility, and bounds the net gain before changing to the comparison lattice. It also allows rationally normalized local exponents. Valuation and length inequalities then determine which integer powers of a uniformizer they imply.

For a concrete chain over $ℤ_3$, take $T_1:ℤ_3^2 arrow.r ℤ_3^3$ with columns $3 e_1,3 e_2$ and $T_2:ℤ_3^3 arrow.r ℤ_3^2$ with columns $9 e_1,9 e_2,0$. Every output of $T_2 T_1$ is divisible by $27$. In the target lattice $L'_2=3ℤ_3 e_1+3ℤ_3 e_2$ this is at least $3^2$-divisibility. The determinant of the composite relative to the original bases is $27^2$; in the new target basis it is $9^2$. If the second map instead kills $e_2$, the common divisibilities persist and the determinant vanishes. Thus the information measured by an exponent and the information measured by the rank have to be supplied independently. The local valuation theorem supplies the former, the common-image estimates control the latter.

Finally, a chain can be studied simultaneously at several finite places because integral lattices are specified by their localizations and a global rational map restricts to each localization. For a rational scalar $c=a/b$ in lowest terms, the assertion that $v_(p)(c)>=0$ for every prime says that $b=1$. The corresponding criterion for a number field uses its ring of integers as the intersection of the discrete valuation rings at finite places. Applied to the matrix coefficients in lattice bases it tests whether the rational map sends one global lattice into another. At the archimedean places there is no comparable divisibility condition. Operator norms, volume, and the heights of the same maps enter there. The determinant comparison in the next chapters works because these are assertions about the same rational period on the chosen coefficient field.

